Droppen server — Linux (@ARCH@, glibc 2.28 or newer)
====================================================

This is the Droppen server, for a Linux device you use from elsewhere: a
server, a VM, a desktop that stays on. The one-line installer does everything
below, runs it as a systemd user service, and adds the device to your Droppen
account with a one-time code (on droppen.app: my devices → add a device that
runs AI → show a one-time code):

    curl -fsSL https://droppen.app/install.sh | DROPPEN_CODE=<code> sh

Droppen was called remote-hand. The server is now `droppen`; the binary its
supervisor runs, `<data>/bin/remote-hand-server`, and its data directory keep
the old name, so an existing install carries on unchanged.

Droppen is licensed under AGPL-3.0-only (see LICENSE); THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES lists
the third-party software in the server and its licences.
Copyright (C) 2026 Kilerd

By hand, as the user Droppen should run as (not root: it runs AI agents and
shells as that user).
@CONTENTS@
Install it as `remote-hand-server` — the supervisor looks for exactly
`<data>/bin/remote-hand-server` — with `droppen` beside it, the command you
type:

    mkdir -p ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin
    mv remote-hand-server ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/
    chmod +x ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/remote-hand-server
    ln -sf remote-hand-server ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/droppen

Create the local login before the first start. Until it has an account the
server shows every browser a setup page: on this device
`~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/droppen open` opens it signed in, and elsewhere
the first account takes the one-time code the server keeps
(`droppen setup-code`). Enrolling below needs an account anyway:

    ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/droppen user add "$(id -un)" --instance default

Add the device to your account. The code goes on standard input, never on the
command line, which other users of this device can read; typed at the prompt
it is not echoed. Keep the droppen.app page that showed the code open while
this waits: it adds the device by itself.

    ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/droppen agent enroll - --hub wss://droppen.app --instance default

It exits 0 once the device is added. 76 means the hub took the code but the
page has not added the device yet: the server finishes joining once it does.

Run it under its supervisor, which is what makes self-update and
restart-survival work:

    APP_INSTANCE=default ~/.local/share/remote-hand/bin/remote-hand-server up -- serve

It listens on 127.0.0.1:8700 only (APP_SERVER__PORT changes the port). Use it
from droppen.app, or through an SSH tunnel to that port. Under systemd, set
KillMode=process: terminals and agent runs outlive a server restart.

Everything else, including how to uninstall:
https://github.com/getdroppen/droppen#readme
